Title: THE FOUNTAIN HOUSE RULES
Credit: Written by
Author: JINI KASTING
Draft date: December 9th, 2025
Notes:
    A short film based on the rules
    of Fountain. Developed by John
    August, Nima Yousefi and Stu
    Maschwitz.

EXT. FOUNTAIN HOUSE RULES CAF**É** - DAY

A charming café on a quiet city street. 

The sign above the door reads: *“Plain Text, Strong Coffee.”*

JINI (late 20s) bright-eyed and restless, sits at a patio table with a notebook open in front of her.  Jini checks her phone. 

No messages. 
 
A WAITRESS approaches, her smile polite but practiced.

WAITRESS  
Can I get you started?

JINI  
Um—just a coffee. Black. Extra black. Like... **bold** text black.

The waitress gives Jini a knowing smile. 

WAITRESS  
Sure. One **bold** coffee coming up.

The waitress exits. 

A shadow crosses Jini's table. She looks up.

JINI  
Konrad, you made it!

KONRAD (30) is tall, dark and... British. His accent is smoooth as butter.

KONRAD
Hello, Jini. Lovely to see you.
 
Konrad sits down across from Jini and looks on her softly.

JINI  
(hesitant)
You're not annoyed at me asking you here?

KONRAD  
Not at all. But you probably could’ve figured the Fountain rules out without me.  
(beat)  
They’re really quite simple.

JINI  
Okay, so where do we start?

KONRAD  
Well, the golden rule of Fountain is: *make it look like a screenplay*.

Jini picks up her notepad and pen and starts writing. [[fountain is easy | id:1051i]]

JINI
Ok, so how do we do that?

KONRAD
Well, we have some very simple syntax rules. First off, scene headings begin with an INT or an EXT.

JINI
Right, *interior* or *exterior*. I get it. Pretty standard stuff.

KONRAD
Should we try it?

JINI
Yeah, for sure. Of course.

KONRAD
Ok let's do it then.

INT. FOUNTAIN HOUSE RULES CAFÉ - DAY

We're transported to inside the café. Jini and Konrad are tucked now into a cozy little corner of the café.

JINI
Yikes!

KONRAD
See, simple enough.

JINI
I guess it is.
(lean in)
So what's next?

KONRAD
Well, character names would be the  next logical element.  And they're simple enough.  It's just the character's name in all uppercase.

JINI
Like this?

KONRAD
Perfect, you've been doing it all along and you didn't even know it. Fountain is very intuitive.

Jini squeals.

JINI
Make it look like a screenplay!

KONRAD
Yeah, it's pretty much that easy.

Jini scribbles some more on her notepad. [[just make it look like a screenplay! | id:22ws9]]

JINI
So then dialogue would be whatever follows the character name?

KONRAD
It's not rocket science is it?

JINI
I thought Fountain was complicated!

KONRAD
Nope.

JINI
So what if I was _really_ mad or something? Or maybe I was super grateful that you even showed up today? We could do that with a parenthetical, right?

KONRAD
You want to try?

JINI
Yes!

KONRAD
Well, I'm not stopping you.

Jini puts a hand across her mouth and begins to tear up.

JINI
(crying)
Konrad, I'm _SO_ happy you met me here today. I can't tell you how much it means to me.

KONRAD
It's my pleasure, Jini.

Jini's tears are suddenly gone.

JINI
I guess it worked! So what's left?

KONRAD
Uhmmm— just transitions.

JINI
(excited again)
Should we try one?

CUT TO:

INT. TRAIN CAR CAFÉ - DAY

We are transported again, this time to an elegant, wood-paneled train car. Brass fixtures, velvet seats, and frosted windows blur the passing countryside.

JINI
Wow, nice choice! This is lux. So that's it? That's all there is to Fountain?

KONRAD
You have the basics now. It's really all you need to write an entire screenplay. If you wanted to get into the nitty-gritty you check out the fountain website.

FADE TO BLACK.